---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { brand } from '../config/brand';
---

<Base
  title="Publishing system"
  description="How the owner-operated Truth to Live By publishing system uses Google and YouTube authorization."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <span class="eyebrow">PUBLISHING WITH CARE</span>
      <h1>
        The Truth to Live By
        <br />
        <em>publishing system.</em>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede">
        A private, owner-operated tool for preparing and publishing approved Truth to Live By
        material across the brand’s official channels.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose-page">
      <aside class="sample-note">
        <strong>Prepared for owner and OAuth review.</strong> This page is part of the local website
        preview and is not yet publicly deployed.
      </aside>

      <h2>What the application does</h2>
      <p>
        The publishing system helps the owner of Truth to Live By upload approved, locally stored
        videos and their accompanying titles, descriptions and other metadata to the official{' '}
        <a href={brand.youtube}>Truth to Live By YouTube channel</a>. It also keeps separate
        publishing records for each configured brand and platform.
      </p>
      <p>
        The application is an internal brand-management tool operated by Mark Walsh, the owner of
        the Truth to Live By project. It is not offered to the public, does not let visitors sign
        in, and does not publish videos belonging to other people.
      </p>

      <h2>How Google and YouTube access is used</h2>
      <p>
        Google OAuth authorisation allows the application to confirm that it is connected to the
        intended YouTube channel, refresh its authorised session and upload a video selected by the
        owner. The current workflow creates uploaded videos as private. Public, unlisted and
        scheduled publishing are blocked until a separate approval and compliance process is
        completed.
      </p>
      <p>
        The application processes the authorised channel’s identifier and title, the video and
        metadata supplied by the owner, and the video identifier and status returned by YouTube. It
        does not ask for or receive the owner’s Google password.
      </p>
      <p>The application requests these Google OAuth permissions:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>youtube.upload</code> — upload the owner-selected videos and their metadata.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>youtube.readonly</code> — read the authorised channel’s basic identity so the
          application can refuse to operate against an unexpected channel.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        These permissions do not allow the application to read the owner’s Gmail, Google Drive or
        unrelated Google Account information.
      </p>

      <h2>Storage and control</h2>
      <p>
        Media, OAuth credentials, publishing state and operational logs remain in the owner’s local
        publishing environment. Credentials and media are excluded from the source repository. The
        public website does not receive OAuth tokens, operate the publisher or store uploaded video
        files.
      </p>

      <h2>Authorisation and withdrawal</h2>
      <p>
        The owner can remove the application’s Google access through the security settings of the
        authorising Google Account. Removing access prevents future token refresh and YouTube
        uploads. Local credentials and publishing records can then be deleted from the publishing
        environment.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy information</h2>
      <p>
        The application uses Google information only to provide the owner-requested channel check
        and video-upload functions. It does not sell Google user data or use it for advertising. See
        the <a href="/privacy/">Truth to Live By Privacy Notice</a> for the information processed,
        its purpose, storage and deletion arrangements, and the contact route for privacy questions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
